# Peerfold keys and base URLs

Apply these to any code, config, or snippet that talks to a Peerfold workspace.

## Two keys, one of which may reach a browser

Both are minted under Settings > Developers in the workspace.

| Key | Where it may live | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `pk_live_...` or `pk_test_...` (publishable) | Browser, page source, a committed theme file | Identifies the workspace. Sent as the `X-Peerfold-Publishable-Key` header. Grants nothing on its own. |
| secret, prefix `sk_` (live or test) | Server environment only | The whole workspace. Sent as `Authorization: Bearer` plus the key. Shown exactly once. |

A secret key never touches a browser, a client bundle, a committed file, or a
prompt. It can mint a learner token for any learner in the workspace
(`POST /api/v1/auth/learner-token`), so anyone holding it can act as anyone. Put
it in the environment as `PEERFOLD_SECRET_KEY`. A leaked key is revoked and
replaced at once.

If you are about to write a secret key into anything a browser loads or Git
tracks, stop and use a different auth model: publishable key, learner PKCE, or a
server-side relay.

The MCP connection this plugin configures uses OAuth and needs no key at all. A
key is only for a program with no browser to sign in through.

## Build against the sandbox

Create a key pair with mode Test. A test secret key resolves to a sandbox workspace
with its own courses, learners and enrollments. It is seeded with demo data and
excluded from CRM sync, transactional email, billing and metering. Nothing done
there can reach production data or email a real person. The MCP `try_endpoint`
tool will only execute a live call under a test key; a live key is refused.

## Two base URLs, and the second one is per workspace

| Plane | Base URL | Credential |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Learner (browser) | `https://your-workspace.peerfold.app` | Short-lived learner JWT (15 minutes) |
| Admin and server | `https://app.peerfold.com` | Secret key |

The learner base URL belongs to the customer's workspace. Never hardcode a
placeholder subdomain into shipped code. Read it from configuration, or from the
`base_url` that `POST /api/v1/connect/wordpress` returns when a site connects
itself. A workspace on a custom domain reads there instead.

## Request conventions

- Pin the contract with `Peerfold-Version: 2026-07-01`.
- Errors are RFC 7807 `application/problem+json` with a stable machine-readable
  `code`. Every response carries `X-Request-Id`. Log it, because it turns a
  support question into a lookup.
- Rate limits: 120 requests per minute per key on Professional, 600 on Enterprise,
  60 per minute for the anonymous cart. Every response carries limit, remaining and
  reset. Back off on a 429.
- A browser origin must be on the workspace's CORS allowlist (Settings >
  Developers) or the browser blocks the response with nothing in any server log
  to read. This is the most common cause of a surface that renders its empty state
  on a correctly configured page.
